            <ArticleTitle>Osteitis condensans ilii in differential diagnosis of patients with chronic low back pain: a review of the literature.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>467-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s10165-011-0513-9</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Osteitis condensans ilii (OCI) is a benign pathology causing chronic back and hip pain. Although the definitive cause is uncertain, mechanical stress is a significant factor in the development of the disease. Bilateral involvement of the sacroiliac joint is typical. We describe a case of unilateral OCI with unilateral sclerosis observed at radiography in a 34-year-old patient presenting with chronic back and hip pain, together with a review of the literature.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
